Revision history for Text-Nunjucks

{{$NEXT}}
  - The round filter with a precision from -22 to 22, and 10 ** n in a
    template for such an n, give JavaScript's result on every system; the
    C library's pow is one unit in the last place off for some of them on
    the BSDs (round(-21, "ceil") printed 999999999999999900000 there).
  - A template name or an include that names a directory throws node's
    "EISDIR: illegal operation on a directory, read" on every system; where
    the system lets a directory be read, its raw entries rendered as the
    template.
  - The error for a template file that cannot be read names the error code
    as node does (ENOENT, EACCES, ...) also where Perl knows the errno by a
    second name; on Strawberry Perl ENOENT read EC_QUERYWAITING.

0.001     2026-11-17 20:12:32Z
  - First release: a Perl port of Nunjucks 3.2.4. The same template,
    context and options are meant to give the same output and the same
    error messages as the JavaScript original. Requires Perl 5.40 or newer.
  - Use Text::Nunjucks::Environment as the entry point. The top-level API of
    Nunjucks' index.js (configure, render, compile, render_string over a
    default environment) is not in this release, so Text::Nunjucks itself
    loads no code.
  - Included: the template pipeline, the built-in filters, the "is" tests, the
    globals (range, cycler, joiner) and the FileSystem and Precompiled
    loaders. Not included: jinja-compat, precompile and its CLI,
    NodeResolveLoader, WebLoader, watch, express and the browser bundles.
  - Numbers are IEEE doubles and print as JavaScript prints them on every
    perl, also with long double or quadmath NVs and on 32-bit perls.
  - Every class lives in its own .pm file and each module loads on its own
    (use Text::Nunjucks::Template, ::Context, ::Runtime::SafeString and so
    on). Loading a parent module (Environment, Nodes, Runtime, ...) loads
    its classes.
  - A render frees its Context when nothing of it is going to run any more,
    also for templates with macros, caller blocks, imports and includes. A
    macro your own Perl code keeps and calls after its render ended dies when
    it reads a template variable.
  - The port sets no depth limit of its own: where Nunjucks stops with a
    RangeError (many asyncEach items, sequential includes, a template that
    includes itself, a macro that calls itself) the port renders, bounded
    only by memory, and an endless recursion runs until memory is exhausted.
  - Paths follow the POSIX model of node's path module; Windows paths
    (path.win32) come later. The tests that rest on the POSIX model are
    skipped on Windows, all others run there.
  - See "DIFFERENCES FROM NUNJUCKS" in the Text::Nunjucks documentation for
    every deliberate and every known open difference, and "SECURITY" before
    passing template names you do not control to the FileSystem loader.
